1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,885
Part Seven assembled a rational oscillating sample and a finite sum
with a proved error budget.

2
00:00:06,408 --> 00:00:11,402
Those computations are tools for comparing two descriptions of the
same weighted reading.

3
00:00:11,924 --> 00:00:15,403
One description comes from a ceiling on the original signal.

4
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:19,744
The other comes from a pole associated with a hypothetical zero.

5
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:26,255
To make the second description usable, we need to isolate a pole and
control everything around it.

6
00:00:26,777 --> 00:00:35,395
This episode follows that chain through finite geometry, coefficient
bounds, and the conditional contradiction already formalized in the
repository.

7
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:43,203
The pictures use deliberately small illustrative examples; they are
not certificates for actual zeros of the zeta function.

8
00:00:43,726 --> 00:00:49,520
This is Part Eight of Landau Windows ACL Two: poles, zero covers,
and the contradiction.

9
00:00:51,667 --> 00:00:55,109
Begin with the function one divided by one minus x.

10
00:00:55,632 --> 00:01:02,044
As x approaches one from the left, the denominator approaches zero
and the output grows without bound.

11
00:01:02,567 --> 00:01:06,664
On the complex plane, the corresponding expression has a pole at
one.

12
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:15,877
A simple pole is a reciprocal singularity: near its location, a
nonzero constant divided by the displacement supplies the singular
part.

13
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,073
What remains after subtracting that part is regular nearby.

14
00:01:20,595 --> 00:01:26,292
Regular means analytic here: it admits a convergent power-series
description in a neighborhood.

15
00:01:26,815 --> 00:01:30,415
We will not calculate a complex integral from this drawing.

16
00:01:30,938 --> 00:01:35,750
We will use the pole's algebraic fingerprint in the coefficients of
a power series.

17
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:42,315
The familiar geometric series can be introduced without taking an
infinite sum.

18
00:01:42,838 --> 00:01:50,421
At x equal to one half, the first three terms are one… one half… and
one quarter.

19
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:52,750
They add to seven quarters.

20
00:01:53,273 --> 00:01:57,430
The remaining correction is one quarter, so the total is exactly
two.

21
00:01:57,953 --> 00:02:06,232
More generally, multiplying a finite geometric prefix by one minus x
cancels the interior terms and leaves one minus the next power.

22
00:02:06,755 --> 00:02:12,003
Dividing by one minus x gives the displayed finite identity,
whenever x is not one.

23
00:02:12,526 --> 00:02:18,295
The teaching function computes the prefix by adding one and
multiplying the shorter prefix by x.

24
00:02:18,818 --> 00:02:27,812
For absolute x less than one, the correction tends to zero; that
final limiting statement is the analytic interpretation of these
finite identities.

25
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:33,048
Move the pole to an offset from the expansion center.

26
00:02:33,571 --> 00:02:38,068
Write its contribution as a constant divided by the offset minus z.

27
00:02:38,591 --> 00:02:42,227
Factor out the offset and apply the same geometric identity.

28
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:49,017
The coefficient of the j-th power of z is the constant divided by
the offset to the power j plus one.

29
00:02:49,539 --> 00:02:57,620
For constant one and offset two, the first coefficients are one
half… one quarter… and one eighth.

30
00:02:58,142 --> 00:03:01,355
The coefficient at index five is one sixty-fourth.

31
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:07,138
These coefficients get smaller, but a farther pole's coefficients
can get smaller faster.

32
00:03:07,661 --> 00:03:12,691
That relative rate will matter more than whether an individual
sequence grows or shrinks.

33
00:03:14,833 --> 00:03:19,233
A complex reciprocal still has an exact rational representation.

34
00:03:19,756 --> 00:03:27,283
For a pair x and y, conjugation reverses the sign of y, and the
squared magnitude is x squared plus y squared.

35
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:34,630
Divide the conjugate by that squared magnitude to obtain the
reciprocal, provided the original point is nonzero.

36
00:03:35,153 --> 00:03:39,868
For the point two plus i, this gives two fifths minus one fifth
times i.

37
00:03:40,391 --> 00:03:47,991
The actual pole-coefficient function multiplies the residue
parameter by the reciprocal offset raised to the requested power
plus one.

38
00:03:48,514 --> 00:03:53,665
The multiplication and finite-power operations are the ones
introduced in Part Seven.

39
00:03:54,188 --> 00:04:03,424
The displayed definition is executable rational code, while its use
as a Taylor coefficient has the stated nonzero-offset and analytic
hypotheses.

40
00:04:03,947 --> 00:04:09,995
The usual nfix convention appears again in the index; our stated
domain uses a natural number.

41
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:18,400
Now consider three rational points, all strictly to the left of the
vertical line through real coordinate two.

42
00:04:18,923 --> 00:04:23,274
They are illustrative pole locations, not actual zeta zeros.

43
00:04:23,797 --> 00:04:28,379
At height zero, the upper and lower points are equally far from our
center.

44
00:04:28,902 --> 00:04:31,229
The squared distance to either is two.

45
00:04:31,752 --> 00:04:36,164
A nearest-point argument with a strict gap cannot simply ignore that
tie.

46
00:04:36,686 --> 00:04:41,826
Move the center upward along the vertical line, and one of those
points becomes closer.

47
00:04:42,348 --> 00:04:47,609
The distance calculation uses only differences of rational
coordinates and their squares.

48
00:04:48,132 --> 00:04:53,562
We will choose the height by a finite construction, rather than
saying to pick a generic point.

49
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:58,755
Compare the squared distances to two fixed points.

50
00:04:59,277 --> 00:05:03,665
The height-squared terms cancel, leaving a linear equation in the
center height.

51
00:05:04,188 --> 00:05:09,533
If the two imaginary coordinates differ, at most one height makes
the distances equal.

52
00:05:10,056 --> 00:05:14,153
The code computes that candidate using a numerator and a
denominator.

53
00:05:14,676 --> 00:05:19,888
In our three-point example, the distinct forbidden heights are minus
one, zero, and one.

54
00:05:20,411 --> 00:05:29,380
If two distinct points have the same imaginary coordinate, their
horizontal distances differ because both points lie strictly to the
left of the center line.

55
00:05:29,903 --> 00:05:33,285
So that case cannot create a distance tie at any height.

56
00:05:33,808 --> 00:05:41,202
The code's zero-denominator branch returns a harmless extra
candidate; avoiding extra heights does not hurt the construction.

57
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:48,242
Start with the interval from minus one to one, and process the
forbidden list in order.

58
00:05:48,765 --> 00:05:54,644
The first midpoint is zero; minus one lies on its lower side, so
keep the upper half.

59
00:05:55,167 --> 00:06:01,300
The next midpoint is one half; zero lies on its lower side, so keep
the upper half again.

60
00:06:01,823 --> 00:06:07,362
The next midpoint is three quarters; one lies on its upper side, so
keep the lower half.

61
00:06:07,885 --> 00:06:11,267
We are left with the interval from one half to three quarters.

62
00:06:11,789 --> 00:06:15,826
Every processed forbidden value is outside its open interior.

63
00:06:16,348 --> 00:06:19,673
Choose its midpoint… five eighths.

64
00:06:20,196 --> 00:06:29,566
The interval still has positive width after finitely many halvings,
so this gives a rational height inside the original interval and
different from every forbidden height.

65
00:06:30,089 --> 00:06:34,428
The source shows both the interval recursion and the final midpoint
wrapper.

66
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:40,106
At height five eighths, compute the three squared distances.

67
00:06:40,629 --> 00:06:49,424
They are seventy-three sixty-fourths… two hundred thirty-three
sixty-fourths… and two hundred eighty-one sixty-fourths.

68
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:52,456
The upper point is now strictly nearest.

69
00:06:52,979 --> 00:06:58,567
The nearest-point function recursively compares the first candidate
with the winner from the rest of the list.

70
00:06:59,089 --> 00:07:04,629
It uses squared distances, so no square-root approximation is needed
for the comparison.

71
00:07:05,152 --> 00:07:10,255
The finite comparison theorem establishes that the returned point
minimizes distance.

72
00:07:10,777 --> 00:07:16,002
The avoided-height argument strengthens this to a strict inequality
for every different point.

73
00:07:16,524 --> 00:07:22,815
A repeated copy of the selected point is the same location, so it is
not treated as a separate competitor.

74
00:07:24,933 --> 00:07:29,442
Take the selected squared distance and the next different point's
squared distance.

75
00:07:29,965 --> 00:07:34,862
Also impose a rational upper cap, if the surrounding argument
requires one.

76
00:07:35,385 --> 00:07:40,052
Average the selected distance with the smaller of that cap and the
next distance.

77
00:07:40,574 --> 00:07:45,811
In this example the resulting squared radius is one hundred fifty-
three sixty-fourths.

78
00:07:46,333 --> 00:07:52,576
It is strictly above seventy-three sixty-fourths and strictly below
two hundred thirty-three sixty-fourths.

79
00:07:53,098 --> 00:07:57,438
The circle therefore contains the selected location and excludes the
others.

80
00:07:57,961 --> 00:08:03,488
The code first removes every equal copy of the selected point, then
computes this midpoint.

81
00:08:04,011 --> 00:08:10,411
The drawing uses the square root only to display the circle; the
certificate stores its squared radius.

82
00:08:10,933 --> 00:08:19,479
This construction requires a nonempty finite point list, the left-
of-center condition, and a cap strictly above the selected squared
distance.

83
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:25,806
An actual zero of the zeta function need not have rational
coordinates.

84
00:08:26,329 --> 00:08:29,602
A rational marker drawn near it is therefore not enough.

85
00:08:30,124 --> 00:08:35,664
Instead, represent uncertainty by a box with rational horizontal and
vertical bounds.

86
00:08:36,186 --> 00:08:43,641
The four entries are the real lower endpoint, real upper endpoint,
imaginary lower endpoint, and imaginary upper endpoint.

87
00:08:44,164 --> 00:08:47,739
The box predicate checks that both intervals are well formed.

88
00:08:48,262 --> 00:08:52,262
The containment predicate checks a rational point against those
intervals.

89
00:08:52,785 --> 00:08:58,785
For actual complex points, the Lean interpretation theorem supplies
the corresponding real meaning.

90
00:08:59,308 --> 00:09:07,198
From now on, the circle must include the whole selected box and
exclude every competing box, rather than merely separating their
centers.

91
00:09:09,333 --> 00:09:13,552
The distance from a center to a box is computed with interval
arithmetic.

92
00:09:14,074 --> 00:09:19,395
There is one detail worth slowing down for: squaring an interval
that crosses zero.

93
00:09:19,918 --> 00:09:24,585
For the interval from minus two to three, squaring the endpoints
gives four and nine.

94
00:09:25,108 --> 00:09:29,738
But zero is also inside the original interval, and its square is
zero.

95
00:09:30,261 --> 00:09:33,485
So the correct squared interval is from zero to nine.

96
00:09:34,008 --> 00:09:39,692
If an interval stays on one side of zero, the smaller endpoint
square supplies the lower bound.

97
00:09:40,215 --> 00:09:44,227
The larger endpoint square supplies the upper bound in either case.

98
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:48,786
The actual square-interval definition makes exactly that
distinction.

99
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:57,567
Subtract the box's real interval from the center's real coordinate,
and do the same for the imaginary coordinate.

100
00:09:58,089 --> 00:10:02,453
Square those two interval differences, then add the resulting
intervals.

101
00:10:02,976 --> 00:10:07,679
The result encloses every squared distance from the center to a
point in the box.

102
00:10:08,202 --> 00:10:12,032
We now use a separate box example with center two plus i.

103
00:10:12,555 --> 00:10:20,979
The selected box is a small rectangle around three fifths plus i,
and the competing box is around its reflection below the real axis.

104
00:10:21,502 --> 00:10:30,168
The exact bounds certify that every selected-box squared distance is
less than two, while every competing-box squared distance is greater
than five.

105
00:10:30,691 --> 00:10:35,855
These are conservative summaries of the exact rational intervals
shown in the code example.

106
00:10:36,377 --> 00:10:40,292
A squared radius of three therefore has room on both sides.

107
00:10:42,433 --> 00:10:50,070
The outside-boxes recursion checks that each box's lower squared-
distance bound is strictly greater than the stored squared radius.

108
00:10:50,592 --> 00:10:56,702
The circle checker also requires a positive rational squared radius
and a valid selected box.

109
00:10:57,224 --> 00:11:03,455
It checks that the selected box's upper squared-distance bound is
strictly smaller than that radius value.

110
00:11:03,977 --> 00:11:11,432
Together these comparisons imply that every point in the selected
box is inside, and every point in the other boxes is outside.

111
00:11:11,955 --> 00:11:19,320
In the worked example, the short summary is two… less than three…
less than five.

112
00:11:19,842 --> 00:11:25,467
The word radius in the ACL2 parameter name refers to the squared
radius in this checker.

113
00:11:25,989 --> 00:11:32,111
Keeping that distinction explicit avoids taking a square root or
comparing quantities with different meanings.

114
00:11:34,233 --> 00:11:38,597
The checker has established a geometric statement about the supplied
boxes.

115
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:43,180
It has not proved that every relevant zero occurs in those boxes.

116
00:11:43,703 --> 00:11:53,073
To isolate an actual zero, we also need a complete cover of the
relevant zero set and a proof that the selected box contains only
the selected zero point.

117
00:11:53,595 --> 00:11:59,292
That point may have multiplicity greater than one; uniqueness of
location is not simplicity.

118
00:11:59,815 --> 00:12:03,427
A missing box could hide another zero inside the circle.

119
00:12:03,950 --> 00:12:09,368
A selected box containing two distinct zeros would also defeat the
intended conclusion.

120
00:12:09,891 --> 00:12:15,539
The Lean circle-isolation theorem carries these coverage and
uniqueness hypotheses explicitly.

121
00:12:16,062 --> 00:12:23,298
Generating and refining suitable complete covers, including
clusters, remains a separate obligation in the full reduction.

122
00:12:25,433 --> 00:12:28,864
Why does a zero produce a pole in the transform at all?

123
00:12:29,386 --> 00:12:36,683
Near a zero of multiplicity m, the logarithmic derivative has
principal part m divided by s minus the zero.

124
00:12:37,206 --> 00:12:41,327
The normalized transform introduces the factor displayed on screen.

125
00:12:41,850 --> 00:12:49,183
The resulting coefficient in the repository's reciprocal-pole
convention is minus m divided by rho times rho minus one.

126
00:12:49,706 --> 00:12:55,548
The source computes that value with rational complex arithmetic when
supplied rational model data.

127
00:12:56,071 --> 00:13:02,374
For the illustrative input one half plus i, multiplicities one and
two give four fifths and eight fifths.

128
00:13:02,897 --> 00:13:06,145
This input is not being asserted to be a zeta zero.

129
00:13:06,668 --> 00:13:14,971
Multiplicity scales the contribution at one location; it does not
create several distinct locations that the geometry must separate.

130
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:20,494
Return to a deliberately simple model with two poles.

131
00:13:21,017 --> 00:13:26,665
Take one divided by two minus z, then subtract three divided by
three minus z.

132
00:13:27,188 --> 00:13:32,218
At index zero, the second contribution is larger and the coefficient
is negative.

133
00:13:32,741 --> 00:13:36,377
So nearest does not mean dominant at every small index.

134
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:41,530
Multiply the coefficient at index j by two to the power j plus one.

135
00:13:42,053 --> 00:13:49,471
The selected contribution becomes one, while the competing
contribution becomes three times two thirds to that same power.

136
00:13:49,994 --> 00:13:57,521
At index five, the normalized coefficient is one hundred seventy-
nine over two hundred forty-three, which is greater than one half.

137
00:13:58,044 --> 00:14:02,444
The farther contribution has become too small to cancel the selected
one.

138
00:14:02,967 --> 00:14:07,767
The teaching function uses the actual pole-coefficient constructor
for both terms.

139
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:15,609
A real pole model also has a regular part, not just the listed
reciprocal terms.

140
00:14:16,132 --> 00:14:29,235
If that regular part is analytic on a sufficiently large disk and
bounded by a constant on its boundary, Cauchy's coefficient estimate
bounds its j-th coefficient by that constant divided by the radius
to the power j.

141
00:14:29,758 --> 00:14:37,006
After normalization by the selected distance, the important factor
is the distance divided by that larger radius.

142
00:14:37,529 --> 00:14:39,347
It is strictly less than one.

143
00:14:39,870 --> 00:14:48,197
The code stores a regular polynomial in a unit-disk variable and
rescales its coefficients by the physical radius to the power minus
j.

144
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:56,259
The finite arithmetic development obtains coefficient caps from
checked polynomial boundary data with a proved extraction allowance.

145
00:14:56,782 --> 00:15:03,097
Sampling a few points of an arbitrary analytic function would not
establish a bound everywhere between them.

146
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:09,535
The polynomial representation and the quantitative extraction
theorem are essential parts of that step.

147
00:15:11,667 --> 00:15:19,970
The actual model coefficient adds three pieces: the selected pole,
the other listed poles, and the rescaled regular coefficient.

148
00:15:20,492 --> 00:15:22,941
The first source card shows that sum.

149
00:15:23,464 --> 00:15:30,955
The model-polynomial constructor then builds a finite list of those
coefficients, advancing the index on each recursive call.

150
00:15:31,477 --> 00:15:36,605
The list is a constructed object, not an assumed agreement with the
Mellin computation.

151
00:15:37,127 --> 00:15:41,697
For fixed rational model inputs, its entries can be computed
exactly.

152
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:47,662
The analytic work must still justify that these inputs describe the
transform we care about.

153
00:15:48,185 --> 00:15:57,130
Keeping construction and interpretation separate lets the proof
checker verify the finite algebra without silently accepting a
missing analytic identity.

154
00:15:59,267 --> 00:16:05,970
The boundary-comparison function first constructs the polynomial
attached to the actual improper Mellin evaluator.

155
00:16:06,492 --> 00:16:12,650
It constructs a pole-model polynomial of the matching degree, then
forms their coefficientwise difference.

156
00:16:13,173 --> 00:16:17,670
The second page checks that difference on the prescribed rational
boundary orbit.

157
00:16:18,192 --> 00:16:24,980
The computed sample count and extraction bounds turn an accepted
check into a bound on the selected coefficient difference.

158
00:16:25,503 --> 00:16:31,006
This is a certificate to be verified, not a claim that every
proposed model will pass.

159
00:16:31,529 --> 00:16:39,080
The numerical improper-polynomial theorem currently uses a disk
wholly inside the region where the real part is greater than two.

160
00:16:39,603 --> 00:16:45,058
Taking a longer cutoff does not itself provide meromorphic
continuation beyond that region.

161
00:16:45,580 --> 00:16:51,508
Connecting suitable analytic models to these comparisons is one of
the remaining mathematical jobs.

162
00:16:53,633 --> 00:17:00,967
Part Five's one-sided inequality contained an extra plain real
reading alongside the magnitude of the oscillating reading.

163
00:17:01,489 --> 00:17:04,338
We cannot erase it when we combine the estimates.

164
00:17:04,861 --> 00:17:09,236
The repository controls that height-zero reading with its own
checked model.

165
00:17:09,759 --> 00:17:18,753
Its bound contains a pole-family mass over a clearance power, a
regular-part cap over a contour-radius power, and explicit
comparison errors.

166
00:17:19,276 --> 00:17:21,918
The displayed function assembles those terms.

167
00:17:22,441 --> 00:17:27,568
A bound on the absolute value of the real reading also bounds how
negative it can be.

168
00:17:28,091 --> 00:17:34,939
That supplies the additional term needed to turn the failed-search
inequality into an upper cap for the oscillating reading.

169
00:17:35,462 --> 00:17:41,305
This is why two compatible models are needed: one at the selected
height and one at height zero.

170
00:17:43,433 --> 00:17:52,524
We now have several small terms to pay for: competing poles, regular
coefficients, the window upper bound, the real anchor, and
comparison errors.

171
00:17:53,047 --> 00:17:57,605
Strict distance-to-radius gaps make the geometric ratios smaller
than one.

172
00:17:58,127 --> 00:18:05,679
The project's geometric threshold machinery computes a sufficiently
large natural index for the prescribed fractions of the budget.

173
00:18:06,202 --> 00:18:11,450
The source shown here combines those requirements for the window,
anchor, and regular terms.

174
00:18:11,973 --> 00:18:19,621
After the index fixes the scale, the model-error precision is one
divided by one hundred twenty-eight times one plus that scale.

175
00:18:20,144 --> 00:18:25,186
Multiplying that tolerance by the scale still gives a small
controlled contribution.

176
00:18:25,709 --> 00:18:32,848
The order matters: caps and clearances must be justified uniformly
before they are used to select an index and a precision.

177
00:18:33,371 --> 00:18:42,195
If the required cap secretly grows when the requested precision
changes, that circular dependency has not been solved by writing
down a threshold.

178
00:18:44,333 --> 00:18:48,891
The final arithmetic pattern is simple once all those estimates are
available.

179
00:18:49,414 --> 00:18:55,571
A selected pole gives a lower bound after subtracting the allowed
cancellation and comparison errors.

180
00:18:56,094 --> 00:19:02,130
Search failure gives an upper bound for that same normalized
reading, using the separate real-anchor estimate.

181
00:19:02,653 --> 00:19:07,926
If the lower bound is strictly larger than the upper bound, no value
can satisfy both.

182
00:19:08,448 --> 00:19:13,115
The teaching theorem on screen states this elementary ordered-
arithmetic obstruction.

183
00:19:13,638 --> 00:19:21,638
The actual project theorem obtains its bounds from the checked
models and computed budgets, rather than inserting the numbers in
our illustration.

184
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:27,215
In that conditional setting, assuming the window search failed leads
to a contradiction.

185
00:19:27,738 --> 00:19:30,295
Therefore the finite search must succeed.

186
00:19:32,433 --> 00:19:37,330
Success is more informative than an abstract statement that some
violation exists.

187
00:19:37,853 --> 00:19:43,683
The conditional witness theorem returns the result of the
repository's actual finite window search.

188
00:19:44,206 --> 00:19:48,594
From that result it computes a rational address inside the intended
window.

189
00:19:49,117 --> 00:19:54,086
The address is at least the window start, and its square is at most
the cube of that start.

190
00:19:54,609 --> 00:20:00,003
That squared comparison expresses the upper endpoint without
computing an irrational square root.

191
00:20:00,526 --> 00:20:04,550
The returned signal interval has a strictly positive lower endpoint.

192
00:20:05,073 --> 00:20:10,745
Its interpretation therefore certifies that the signal exceeds the
proposed ceiling at that address.

193
00:20:11,268 --> 00:20:18,177
Every accepted model, coverage statement, and parameter condition
remains attached to the route that produced this conclusion.

194
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:25,548
We have followed the conditional chain from finite geometry to a
returned arithmetic witness.

195
00:20:26,071 --> 00:20:30,338
The geometry algorithms and box checker are certified constructions.

196
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:37,079
The model comparison and coefficient-budget theorems are certified
implications with explicit premises.

197
00:20:37,602 --> 00:20:46,220
What remains for the full Landau reduction is to supply suitable
analytic models and complete zero covers for the general
mathematical situation.

198
00:20:46,742 --> 00:20:55,579
Their caps must remain compatible as precision changes, and the
final encoded statement still needs its intended Peano Arithmetic
interpretation.

199
00:20:56,102 --> 00:21:01,823
These are substantial proof obligations, not missing labels on an
otherwise finished theorem.

200
00:21:02,345 --> 00:21:10,891
Exposing the finite chain is valuable because it identifies exactly
which work is already arithmetic and exactly where further demotion
is needed.

201
00:21:11,414 --> 00:21:16,965
This episode does not announce a completed proof of the Riemann
Hypothesis in Peano Arithmetic.

202
00:21:19,100 --> 00:21:25,427
The next milestone is the completed reduction, with its hypotheses
supplied and its arithmetic meaning audited.

203
00:21:25,950 --> 00:21:29,744
We will make that final episode when the proof evidence supports it.

204
00:21:30,267 --> 00:21:43,272
For now, the result is a concrete route: checked local geometry…
constructed coefficient models… verified comparisons… and a
conditional finite witness.

205
00:21:43,795 --> 00:21:48,583
Each step can be inspected independently and connected through
stated hypotheses.

206
00:21:49,105 --> 00:21:56,766
That is how Landau Windows ACL Two turns a large foundational
question into smaller, reviewable mathematical tasks.
